From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: the GPU's power domain is GPU_TOP alone

GPU_CORE is not switched by the PCK-600 on this SoC, so the node stops asking it
to be.

WHAT THE SILICON DOES (the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-07)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GPU_CORE switches when three conditions hold at once, with no PPU write anywhere
in the sequence: PLL_GPU0 enabled (0x020020e0), GPU0_CLK ungated with its source
committed through the UPD bit (0x02002b20), and GPU0_GATING with the bus gate on
and the reset released (0x02002b24). Established in three different orders across
three cold boots, the domain switched at the third element every time. Any two of
the three leave PWSR at 0x0, and a policy write -- edge or not, with or without
DEV_REQ_EN and DEV_ACTIVE_EN in PWCR -- never moves it at all, while the same
driver switches domain 0 on demand in the same boot.

Those three conditions are exactly what this driver's own consumer establishes:
clk "core", "mem" and "sys" plus the reset, in pvr_power.c:262-284, which runs in
the runtime-resume callback before the first register access
(pvr_device.c:611 pm_runtime_resume_and_get, then :503 pvr_load_gpu_id ->
ROGUE_CR_CORE_ID__PBVNC). The domain therefore comes up on its own, in time, as
a side effect of the driver doing what it does anyway.

WHY LISTING IT WAS ACTIVELY HARMFUL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
With GPU_CORE in the list, genpd owns a domain it cannot drive. Two shapes of
that were tried on the board and both are bad:

 - keep the PWSR poll (`pmdomain-sunxi-pck600-read-the-boot-state`): power_on() writes the policy and waits 10 ms for a
   status bit that only appears once the consumer has clocked the block, which it
   may only do after power_on() returns. -110, every boot, and the probe that
   would have powered the domain never runs.
 - skip the poll (tried and withdrawn): the attach succeeds, and the board then
   stops silently at 10.7 s with no powervr line on the console. ⚠ The mechanism
   was never isolated. Two candidates fit the same observation and were not
   separated: the driver reaching the block before the domain is up, and
   pm_runtime_put() with the driver's 50 ms autosuspend reaching power_off() on a
   domain that is genuinely in use. The second is in tension with an earlier
   measurement on the board, which found that a PWPR write moves GPU_CORE in neither
   direction and would therefore be inert. What is measured is the outcome, not
   the cause: the same kernel with modprobe.blacklist=powervr reaches
   graphical.target, and a hand modprobe in that boot kills it one console
   marker later.

Dropping the entry removes both. drm/powervr takes exactly one domain without
complaint -- pvr_power.c:625 returns early for domain_count <= 1 and the driver
core attaches the single one itself (pmdomain/core.c:3275, power_on = true) --
and `pmdomain-sunxi-pck600-read-the-boot-state` gives GPU_TOP GENPD_FLAG_ALWAYS_ON because it really is on at boot, so
neither GPU domain sees a PWPR write in either direction. That is the guarantee
upstream buys with GENPD_FLAG_ALWAYS_ON on GPU_CORE (b462a227d854), reached here
without declaring an unpowered domain to be powered.

⚠ Not a free lunch: nothing in the tree now describes GPU_CORE. That is the
honest state -- it is a domain this controller cannot switch -- but it means a
future consumer that expects genpd to power it will find nothing, and the comment
below says so.

VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- applies: patch -p1 --fuzz=0 after `arm64-dts-a733-add-the-gpu-node-disabled`, `arm64-dts-a733-enable-the-gpu-at-the-vendor-s-operating-point` and `arm64-dts-a733-gpu-mem-clock-is-the-gpu-pll`.
- builds: a test build of this series -- the kernel that produced the HW result below.
- HW: proven 2026-09-07 on the 8 GiB board. The falsifier was fixed in advance
  -- powervr must bind with no register write from userspace, reach "Initialized
  powervr", and produce /dev/dri/renderD128 -- and it is met with nothing applied
  by hand and no modprobe.blacklist on the command line: "[drm] Initialized
  powervr 1.0.0 for 1800000.gpu on minor 1", /dev/dri/renderD128 present,
  GPU_CORE PWSR = 0x8, PLL_GPU0 = 0xC9104500. The provider still names the domain
  it cannot switch ("GPU_CORE" is not powered at boot) and the probe no longer
  sees -110. A by-hand session on the board preceded this run.
- A console log of that run was captured.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -2785,9 +2785,16 @@
 				 <&ccu CLK_BUS_GPU0>;
 			clock-names = "core", "mem", "sys";
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_GPU0>;
-			power-domains = <&pck600 PD_GPU_TOP>,
-					<&pck600 PD_GPU_CORE>;
-			power-domain-names = "a", "b";
+			/*
+			 * GPU_TOP only, and that is a statement about the
+			 * silicon: the PCK-600 does not switch GPU_CORE. That
+			 * domain follows the block -- it comes up when
+			 * PLL_GPU0 runs, GPU0_CLK is ungated with its source
+			 * committed through UPD, and the bus gate is on with
+			 * the reset released, which is what this driver's
+			 * runtime resume does before it touches a register.
+			 */
+			power-domains = <&pck600 PD_GPU_TOP>;
 			/*
 			 * ⚠ THE PARENT IS PINNED AND THAT IS A SAFETY
 			 * PROPERTY, NOT A PREFERENCE. Left alone, GPU0_CLK
